  "slug": "adna-alignment-sensitivity-dbd7",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and journal articles report seeding, indexing, and compression designs matched to specific bottlenecks in sequence analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is engineering a data structure or hardware pipeline around one specific cost in sequence processing, rather than a general speedup claim. The SBWT-based preprint targets streaming 31-mer queries through batched, prefetching access over an index that stores a single orientation, reported at up to 4.9x faster than SSHash at 12 bits per canonical k-mer. AutoFFinder maps multi-guide CRISPR/Cas9 off-target search onto a runtime-reconfigurable FPGA platform on hg38, reported faster than GPU and CPU baselines. SigComp addresses a different bottleneck, storage of nanopore raw signal, using an integer-quantized MLP with a GPU-native range coder that outperforms VBZ and ex-zd. The items are heterogeneous, so the common thread is dominant rather than universal: each contribution is a design matched to one cost.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
